{"title":"2018 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 14","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(Exponential_Form)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (Exponential Form)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The solution to the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/4/0646c93115b342379f4f46f3f4579efcc8bc3d7d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\log_{3x} 4 = \\log_{2x} 8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"125\" height=\"17\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is a positive real number other than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/1/4e1965f8d70832ded66434c75a7bd52b75090af0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />, can be written as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/4/2846db355f537b5e50a721c7a83fe61e1f4a9084.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {p}{q}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"11\" height=\"36\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> are relatively prime positive integers. What is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/6/856ee48ce76ddbd106a05384c2ea2c28e3b76c02.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p + q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"41\" height=\"14\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/5/df5b7d6a13a5980700c9cd57735de78897abcf5a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 5   \\qquad     \\textbf{(B) } 13   \\qquad     \\textbf{(C) } 17   \\qquad    \\textbf{(D) } 31 \\qquad   \\textbf{(E) } 35$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"389\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We apply the Change of Base Formula, then rearrange:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/c/19cc17bc94de49d579c72b73d9bd0b23d7b818dc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\frac{\\log_2{4}}{\\log_2{(3x)}}&amp;=\\frac{\\log_2{8}}{\\log_2{(2x)}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\log_2{(3x)}}&amp;=\\frac{3}{\\log_2{(2x)}} \\\\ 3\\log_2{(3x)}&amp;=2\\log_2{(2x)}. \\\\ \\end{align*}\" width=\"186\" height=\"114\" />\nBy the logarithmic identity <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/e/80e79229181adf9a8dae0bfebe43ba7805ef6331.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\log_b{a}=\\log_b{\\left(a^n\\right)},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"154\" height=\"20\" /> it follows that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/8/ba8aafd38ffd307e1e71c416b8af0e3dba6bdeda.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\log_2{\\left[(3x)^3\\right]}&amp;=\\log_2{\\left[(2x)^2\\right]} \\\\ (3x)^3&amp;=(2x)^2 \\\\ 27x^3&amp;=4x^2 \\\\ x&amp;=\\frac{4}{27}, \\end{align*}\" width=\"200\" height=\"120\" />\nfrom which the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/d/e3d8c31c97b34206508942d7fc695458d9fbfd14.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4+27=\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 31}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"146\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~jeremylu (Fundamental Logic)\n</p><p>~MRENTHUSIASM (Reconstruction)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We will apply the following logarithmic identity:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/3/c033090df6e4f09f52a5d1d23776c07cddff6999.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\log_{p^n}{\\left(q^n\\right)}=\\log_{p}{q},\\]\" width=\"145\" height=\"20\" />\nwhich can be proven by the Change of Base Formula: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/b/21b50f64d9c9253a192a39087d5d243d628327cd.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\log_{p^n}{\\left(q^n\\right)}=\\frac{\\log_{p}{\\left(q^n\\right)}}{\\log_{p}{\\left(p^n\\right)}}=\\frac{n\\log_{p}{q}}{n}=\\log_{p}{q}.\\]\" width=\"324\" height=\"46\" />\nWe rewrite the original equation as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/1/e9154fd2c79108f64cb497587e05508ca352aea3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\log_{(3x)^3} 64 = \\log_{(2x)^2} 64,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -8px\" width=\"184\" height=\"22\" /> from which\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/2/b62135a358a811d2e67e0950a716917b9b87a67a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} (3x)^3&amp;=(2x)^2 \\\\ 27x^3&amp;=4x^2 \\\\ x&amp;=\\frac{4}{27}. \\end{align*}\" width=\"103\" height=\"92\" />\nTherefore, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/d/e3d8c31c97b34206508942d7fc695458d9fbfd14.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4+27=\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 31}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"146\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~MRENTHUSIASM\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>By the logarithmic identity <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/e/80e79229181adf9a8dae0bfebe43ba7805ef6331.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\log_b{a}=\\log_b{\\left(a^n\\right)},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"154\" height=\"20\" /> the original equation becomes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/5/1c5c70a897b56af2458b5b5b6f716c17faf110d8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2\\log_{3x} 2 = 3\\log_{2x} 2.\\]\" width=\"152\" height=\"17\" />\nBy the logarithmic identity <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/d/1ed091892c6770f5966b9dc45e56e253fab6fee7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\log_b{a}\\cdot\\log_a{b}=1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"138\" height=\"19\" /> we multiply both sides by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/8/f48438914c307876b155509ebf645cea87a33b23.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\log_2{(2x)},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"74\" height=\"20\" /> then apply the Change of Base Formula to the left side:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/8/f08819a5b13f334ac33caa20838dfcb62db2cdd6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} 2\\left[\\log_{3x}2\\right]\\left[\\log_2{(2x)}\\right] &amp;= 3 \\\\ 2\\left[\\frac{\\log_2 2}{\\log_2{(3x)}}\\right]\\left[\\frac{\\log_2{(2x)}}{\\log_2 2}\\right] &amp;= 3 \\\\ 2\\left[\\frac{\\log_2{(2x)}}{\\log_2{(3x)}}\\right] &amp;=3 \\\\ 2\\left[\\log_{3x}{(2x)}\\right] &amp;= 3 \\\\ \\log_{3x}{\\left[(2x)^2\\right]} &amp;= 3 \\\\ (3x)^3&amp;=(2x)^2 \\\\ 27x^3&amp;=4x^2 \\\\ x&amp;=\\frac{4}{27}. \\end{align*}\" width=\"259\" height=\"272\" />\nTherefore, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/d/e3d8c31c97b34206508942d7fc695458d9fbfd14.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4+27=\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 31}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"146\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~Pikachu13307 (Fundamental Logic)\n</p><p>~MRENTHUSIASM (Reconstruction)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can convert both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/a/32a35631e1e126934ead6d2af161e9d250827bbb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^2$\" width=\"15\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/2/7d2e5497d2fd16a18da58f6475061f823c4ed9eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^3,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"20\" /> respectively: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/b/b7ba1c715fd314a99da595be6ae7329743cf6448.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2\\log_{3x} (2) = 3\\log_{2x} (2).\\]\" width=\"174\" height=\"18\" />\nConverting the bases of the right side, we get\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/4/e84996d8b1371d594baa855345c134f1d0a45155.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\log_{2x} 2 &amp;= \\frac{\\ln 2}{\\ln (2x)} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot\\log_{3x} (2) &amp;= \\frac{\\ln 2}{\\ln (2x)} \\\\ 2^\\frac{2}{3} &amp;= (3x)^\\frac{\\ln 2}{\\ln (2x)} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\ln 2 &amp;= \\frac{\\ln 2}{\\ln (2x)} \\cdot \\ln (3x). \\end{align*}\" width=\"228\" height=\"170\" />\nDividing both sides by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/b/14b252b34efe69da144b5c5da68ac27399c8a01a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ln 2,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" /> we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/d/6edcfd7e6c605fc41223a04e321b0ffe8ad48b1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{\\ln (3x)}{\\ln (2x)},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"97\" height=\"45\" /> from which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/4/b84f76257312229b7ea2c1460523b36684f0e43e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2\\ln (2x) = 3\\ln (3x).\\]\" width=\"149\" height=\"18\" />\nExpanding this equation gives\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/3/793a79c57882d4cd0275df6ad099f1831db77b35.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} 2\\ln 2 + 2\\ln (x) &amp;= 3\\ln 3 + 3\\ln (x) \\\\ \\ln (x) &amp;= 2\\ln 2 - 3\\ln 3. \\end{align*}\" width=\"249\" height=\"43\" />\nThus, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/a/a9aab51673c3f854bdf34251bc2ea5aa3fc5e427.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x = e^{2\\ln 2 - 3\\ln 3} = \\frac{e^{2\\ln 2}}{e^{3\\ln 3}} = \\frac{2^2}{3^3} = \\frac{4}{27},\\]\" width=\"273\" height=\"39\" />\nfrom which the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/d/e3d8c31c97b34206508942d7fc695458d9fbfd14.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4+27=\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 31}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"146\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~lepetitmoulin (Solution)\n</p><p>~MRENTHUSIASM (Reformatting)\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28Exponential_Form.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(Exponential_Form)\">Solution 5 (Exponential Form)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (Exponential Form)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/4/6944e3719c47d14ff64e86a6db01111d7afd7c2e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=\\log_{3x} 4 = \\log_{2x} 8.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"166\" height=\"19\" /> We convert the equations with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> to the exponential form:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/4/6440625ee4341bb7867af505891f3890834ceb3a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} (3x)^y&amp;=4, \\\\ (2x)^y&amp;=8. \\end{align*}\" width=\"77\" height=\"43\" />\nCubing the first equation and squaring the second equation, we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/9/8a97d14e44ab8dfe7949d70d847771f4d19060c1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} (3x)^{3y}&amp;=64, \\\\ (2x)^{2y}&amp;=64. \\end{align*}\" width=\"93\" height=\"47\" />\nApplying the Transitive Property, we get \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/6/dc6425b7735a4e7f8b4dc5589530abbbdbb6b4da.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} (3x)^{3y}&amp;=(2x)^{2y} \\\\ (3x)^3&amp;=(2x)^2 \\\\ 27x^3&amp;=4x^2 \\\\ x&amp;=\\frac{4}{27}, \\end{align*}\" width=\"117\" height=\"120\" />\nfrom which the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/d/e3d8c31c97b34206508942d7fc695458d9fbfd14.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4+27=\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 31}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"146\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~MRENTHUSIASM\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_12A\" title=\"2018 AMC 12A\">2018 AMC 12A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_12A_Problems\" title=\"2018 AMC 12A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_12A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2018 AMC 12A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" 